    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#adalbero-of-reims"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adalbero-of-reims.jpg" alt="Adalbero of Reims" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#adalbero-of-reims">Adalbero of Reims</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sacre_Hugues_Capet_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#west-francia">West Francia</a>, the western share of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-verdun">Treaty of Verdun</a> in 843. After the last Carolingian king, Louis V, died in a hunting accident in 987, the great nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishops</a>, led by Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#adalbero-of-reims">Adalbero of Reims</a>, elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a>, duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, and he was crowned in July 987. The Carolingian claimant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-duke-of-lower-lorraine">Charles of Lorraine</a> resisted until he was captured in 991. Hugh's real power was modest: the royal domain was a narrow strip around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#orleans">Orléans</a>, while the dukes of Normandy, Aquitaine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a> and the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flanders">Flanders</a>, Champagne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toulouse">Toulouse</a> owed only loose homage. The early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#capetian-dynasty">Capetians</a> survived by holding on to what they had. They had their eldest sons crowned in their own lifetime, a practice that lasted until Philip II, so that the succession never returned to election, and until 1316 every king was followed by a son. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-vi-of-france">Louis VI the Fat</a> (r. 1108–1137), advised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suger">Abbot Suger</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basilica-of-saint-denis">Saint-Denis</a>, subdued the defiant castellans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a> and made the domain safe to travel. The anointing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#reims">Reims</a>, the cult of Saint-Denis and the kings' claimed power to heal scrofula by touch gave the dynasty a sacred standing that its armies could not yet match.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#bordeaux"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bordeaux.jpg" alt="Bordeaux" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#bordeaux">Bordeaux</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bordeaux_Place_de_la_Bourse_de_nuit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mariette, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> was the most populous kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin-christendom">Latin Europe</a>: perhaps sixteen to twenty million people around 1300 within its borders of the time, roughly twenty million by 1700 and some twenty-eight million in 1789. The great majority were peasants growing grain, with wine in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#loire">Loire</a> valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#burgundy">Burgundy</a>, under seigneurs who collected dues and held rights of justice long after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#serfdom">serfdom</a> had largely disappeared. Society was described as three orders, clergy, nobility and the third estate, the last including both rich townspeople and the rural poor. In the twelfth and thirteenth centuries the fairs of Champagne linked Flemish cloth makers with Italian merchants, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a>, with perhaps 200,000 inhabitants around 1300 on some estimates, was the largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> Christendom. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-death">Black Death</a> from 1348 and the wars that followed cut the population sharply before a long recovery after 1450. The Catholic Church was wealthy and powerful; the Protestant minority, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huguenots">Huguenots</a>, was tolerated from 1598 but lost that toleration in 1685, when many fled abroad. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jean-baptiste-colbert">Jean-Baptiste Colbert</a> the crown fostered royal manufactures such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gobelins-manufactory">Gobelins</a> tapestry works and backed projects such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#canal-du-midi">Canal du Midi</a>, finished in 1681. In the eighteenth century Bordeaux and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nantes">Nantes</a> grew rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#coffee-economy">coffee</a> and enslaved Africans, centred on the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-bouvines"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-bouvines.jpg" alt="Battle of Bouvines" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Bouvines_gagnee_par_Philippe_Auguste.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horace Vernet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#capetian-dynasty">Capetians</a> fought with small forces of household knights and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassals</a>, but by the thirteenth century royal armies could defeat rivals outright. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-ii-of-france">Philip II Augustus</a> conquered Normandy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a> in 1202–1204 and at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-bouvines">Battle of Bouvines</a> in 1214 routed Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#otto-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Otto IV</a> and his Flemish allies. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> with England (1337–1453) began disastrously: English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#archery">archers</a> destroyed the French cavalry at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-crecy">Battle of Crécy</a> in 1346, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-ii-of-france">King John II</a> was captured at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-poitiers-1356">Battle of Poitiers</a> in 1356, and defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-agincourt">Battle of Agincourt</a> followed in 1415. Recovery came with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a>'s relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#orleans">Orléans</a> in 1429 and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-vii-holy-roman-emperor">Charles VII</a>'s reforms, which in 1445 created the <em>compagnies d'ordonnance</em>, the first permanent royal cavalry, and used artillery to win at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-formigny">Formigny</a> in 1450 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-castillon">Castillon</a> in 1453. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italian-wars">Italian Wars</a> from 1494 French kings fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, winning at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-marignano">Battle of Marignano</a> in 1515 but losing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francis-i">Francis I</a> as a prisoner at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pavia">Battle of Pavia</a> in 1525. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> the army grew to several hundred thousand men on paper, organised by the war ministers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#michel-le-tellier">Michel Le Tellier</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francois-michel-le-tellier-marquis-de-louvois">Louvois</a>, while Vauban ringed the frontiers with bastioned forts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jean-baptiste-colbert">Colbert</a> built a navy based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brest-france">Brest</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toulon">Toulon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rochefort-charente-maritime">Rochefort</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> usually held the seas.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#louis-ix-duke-of-bavaria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-ix-duke-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Louis IX, Duke of Bavaria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#louis-ix-duke-of-bavaria">Louis IX, Duke of Bavaria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amalia_von_Sachsen_und_Ludwig_IX._(Bayern).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gliwi, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Royal government grew out of the king's household. Philip II created baillis and seneschals, salaried officials who ran justice and finance in the provinces, and kept written records of royal rights. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-ix-duke-of-bavaria">Louis IX</a> the king's court produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#parlement-of-paris">Parlement of Paris</a>, the supreme court of appeal, and a Chamber of Accounts followed in the early fourteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV the Fair</a> (r. 1285–1314) and his lawyers held that the king was emperor in his own kingdom, defied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> and summoned the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#estates-general-france">Estates-General</a> in 1302. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> gave the crown permanent taxes, above all the taille and the salt tax, the gabelle, levied without the estates' consent from the 1440s. Offices were sold and from 1604 became hereditary on payment of an annual fee, the <em>paulette</em>, creating a large, privileged class of officeholders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a>, chief minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII</a> from 1624 to 1642, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jules-mazarin">Cardinal Mazarin</a> after him extended the use of intendants, royal commissioners in the provinces, and Mazarin survived the revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#the-fronde">Fronde</a> (1648–1653). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> then governed personally from 1661 with a small council and no chief minister, gathered the high nobility at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#versailles">Versailles</a>, where the court settled in 1682, and never summoned the Estates-General, which did not meet between 1614 and 1789. In practice royal power remained limited by provincial estates, the parlements, privilege and custom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#basilica-of-saint-denis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilica-of-saint-denis.jpg" alt="Basilica of Saint-Denis" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#basilica-of-saint-denis">Basilica of Saint-Denis</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint-Denis_-_Fa%C3%A7ade.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thomas Clouet, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> set many of the patterns of European monarchy and culture. Gothic architecture began in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suger">Abbot Suger</a>'s rebuilding of the abbey church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#basilica-of-saint-denis">Saint-Denis</a> in the 1140s and spread from cathedrals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chartres">Chartres</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#reims">Reims</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#amiens">Amiens</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#university-of-paris">University of Paris</a> made the city the centre of scholastic theology, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> taught in the thirteenth century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-ix-duke-of-bavaria">Louis IX</a>, canonised in 1297, became a model of Christian kingship. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ordinance-of-villers-cotterets">Ordinance of Villers-Cotterêts</a> of 1539 made French the language of law and administration. In the seventeenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rene-descartes">Descartes</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pierre-corneille">Corneille</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#moliere">Molière</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jean-racine">Racine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#academie-francaise">Académie française</a> founded in 1635 and the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#versailles">Versailles</a> made French taste the standard of European elites, and French increasingly replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> as the language of diplomacy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#montesquieu">Montesquieu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#voltaire">Voltaire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jean-jacques-rousseau">Rousseau</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#denis-diderot">Diderot</a>'s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a></em> was largely conducted in French. Overseas, the kingdom founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-france">New France</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quebec">Quebec</a> in 1608, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louisiana">Louisiana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a> colonies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caribbean">Caribbean</a>, leaving French-speaking communities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#canada">Canada</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#haiti">Haiti</a>. The centralised state built by the kings, with its intendants, permanent army and capital-centred government, was inherited and strengthened by the Revolution and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#napoleon">Napoleon</a>, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alexis-de-tocqueville">Alexis de Tocqueville</a> argued in <em>The Old Regime and the Revolution</em> (1856).</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#storming-of-the-bastille"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/storming-of-the-bastille.jpg" alt="Storming of the Bastille" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#storming-of-the-bastille">Storming of the Bastille</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anonymous_-_Prise_de_la_Bastille.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unidentified painter, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The monarchy's end was driven above all by debt and a failure of reform. The wars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> left heavy debts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#john-law">John Law</a>'s paper-money scheme collapsed in 1720, and the Seven Years' War cost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#canada">Canada</a> and most of its position in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-paris-1763">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1763. Support for the American War of Independence (1778–1783) avenged that defeat but pushed the crown towards bankruptcy while the privileged orders and the parlements resisted new taxes. In 1787 the finance minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-alexandre-de-calonne">Calonne</a> put a reform plan to an Assembly of Notables, which rejected it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> agreed to summon the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#estates-general-france">Estates-General</a>, which met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#versailles">Versailles</a> in May 1789. The third estate proclaimed itself the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> in June, the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#storming-of-the-bastille">Bastille</a> on 14 July protected it from royal troops, and in August it abolished feudal privileges and issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#declaration-of-the-rights-of-man-and-of-the-citizen">Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</a>. The king's attempted flight to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#flight-to-varennes">Varennes</a> in June 1791 destroyed trust in him, though the Constitution of 1791 kept him as a constitutional monarch. War with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#austria">Austria</a> from April 1792 and fear of betrayal led to the storming of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tuileries-palace">Tuileries</a> on 10 August 1792; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#national-convention">National Convention</a> abolished the monarchy on 21 September 1792, and Louis XVI was guillotined in January 1793.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#france">France</a> turned a small royal domain around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paris">Paris</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>'s most populous and centralised monarchy, shaping European government, Gothic art, scholastic learning, court culture and the international standing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#french-language">French language</a>, before debt and failed reform brought the Revolution that abolished it in 1792.</em></p>

